 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed changes are pushed to connected browsers over sockets the project operates. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the simplest stateless operation but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag to a board whose purpose is fast status visibility. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it reaches low latency with less connection work but adds recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting accepts connection capacity and reconnect obligations in exchange for fast updates without a realtime vendor. See [the delivery decision](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md).
